		  Chemtab Teacher's User Manual Version 1.2			 Effective August 4th, 1990-------------------------------------------------------------------------------	Chemtab is set up to be easy to use.  The ease is first seen by howeasy it is to start the program.  To start, sit down at a terminal.  Youshould see an information message followed by the prompt ``login: ''.At this prompt, type 'chemtab', hit RETURN, and you may begin to use Chemtab.After you read the title page, hit SPACE BAR.	The program will ask you if you want extra explanations (seen on thebottom line of the screen).  This question asks you if you would like theprogram to put information after every question it asks you about what youshould type.  Answer by typing 'y' or 'n'.	After answering, the program will them ask you if you want a printedtranscript of your session.  Simply put, if you answer yes, the program willsave every graph, list, and chart you produce using it.  If you answer yes,then you must type in your last	name so that when you pick up the printout,it will have your name on it and you know which is yours.  If you answeryes, after every graph, chart or list you produce the program will ask youif you would like to add it to the transcript.  If you accidentally hit thewrong key and got the wrong graph, you can type 'n' when it asks you thisquestion.	Now you are ready to begin using the real fun part of the program.Before you, you should see the MAIN MENU.  To choose which part of theprogram you would like to use, type in the number corresponding to thechoice on the screen.  The first choice is "Look at a single element" orsomething to that effect.  If you type '1', you will see another screen.It will ask you to choose which element to look at by Atomic Number, Symbol,or Name.  Choose which one you want by typing in the number next to thechoice and answer the question the program asks you.  Then, the program willdisplay everything it knows about the element.  Follow the directions at thebottom of the screen to get back to the MAIN MENU.	At the MAIN MENU, at the top of the screen, you will see a linesimilar to this:		'Selection Characteristics: (max of 3)'The space below this line will tell you if you have set any selections forthe computer to look through the list of elements for.  As you can see, youmay have up to three.  To make/change one, select mnu number '2'.The screen will change and give you a list of characteristics.  Say, forexample, you wanted to look for every element with an atomic mass under 40.You would choose Atomic Mass by typing in the number next to it, and hitRETURN.  Then the program would ask you if you want to make Atomic MassEqual to, Greater Than, Less Than, or Close To.  You would choose Less thanby hitting the number next to it.  Then, the program will say:'You would like to look for elements with an Atomic Mass Less Than:''What Value? 'so you type in the value 40 and hit RETURN.  Now you will be at thecharacteristics page and the selection you just made will be at the top ofthe screen.  If you wanted to erase what you just did, follow the sameinstructions as above a second time.  The program will ask you if you wantto change the current selection of Atomic Mass.  Type 'y' to change it.Now, instead of choosing 'Less Than', you would choose, 'Exit [will erasecharacteristic]'.  The selection will disappear.  If you want to erase allof them at once, type '15' and RETURN at the characteristics screen.  Menuoption 15 is 'Erase all Characteristics'.  Type y to confirm that you wantto do this.	If you wanted to view all the elements with an Atomic Mass between 25 and40, you would follow the above steps.  Then you would type in the number forAtomic Mass again.  The program will ask you if you wish to change thecurrent selection of Atomic Mass.  Type 'n'.  Then it will ask you if youwant to make a second characteristic of the same type.  Type 'y'.  Theselect Greater Than and type in 25.  Now, the top of the screen will saythat you are looking for elements with an Atomic Mass Greater Than 25 ANDLess Than 40.  You are ready to move on the the next step.  Choose number 14to return to the main menu.  As you return to the main menu, the elementsthat you are looking for are found and stored in the computer's memory foryou.	At the MAIN MENU, if you would like to list the elements you juststored in menory, type '3'.  If you would like to see where those elementsare on the periodic table of the elements, hit '4'.  If you would like tograph, type '5'.  To quit, type '6'.	Graphing was the hardest part of the program and also the mostpleasing to finish.  You can graph any characteristic of an element againstanother with this program.  This allows the you to see a pattern in theelements.  Say, for example, you wanted to see the pattern ofelectronegativity in the first 40 elements.  If you have selected certainelements, the program will ask you if you want to use those elements, OR ifyou want to see all the elements.  Type 'a' for the first, or 'b' for thesecond.  You should type 'b' now.  The program will now ask you to choosewhat to graph with.  Type the letter corresponding to Atomic Number.Near the bottom of the screen, the computer will tell you it wants to knowwhat range to use.  If you wanted to see ALL of the elements, you would hitRETURN here.. but we only want to see 1 through 40, so type '1' and RETURN.Then type '40' and RETURN.  Now you choose what to graph against.  Choosethe letter for electronegativity.  Now, the computer will make a graph foryou.  See the patterns?	You could do this another way.  Use the selection options (main menucommand '2') to store in memory all elements with an atomic number less than40.  Then choose '5' for graphing at the main menu.  Choose 'a' to use thestored elements.  Choose Atomic Number, and hit RETURN for Minimum value(this means you will use every element) Then choose electronegativity.  Theprogram will only print out the elements it has stored in memory.  There youhave it!	A new feature was added in the last minute of production.  You maymake a characteristic which is not Equal, Greater or Less Than.  The new oneis 'Close To'.  See appendix A for a list of values.  What 'Close To' will dois select elements within a boundary preset by myself, the author, of plusof minus that amount to what you type.  For example, electronegativity has a'Close To' value of 0.20.  This means if you chose Close To 1.5, any elementwith an electronegativity from (1.5 - 0.20) - (1.5 + 0.20) or 1.3 to 1.7 willbe stored in memory.Chemtab Appendix A - List of Close To Values:Atomic Number - 5	Atomic Mass - 10	Family - 1Row	      - 1	Valence El. - 1		Melt Temp - 100 DegreesBoil Temp     - 100 Dg	Ionization Energy - 25	Year of Discovery - 25 yearsSpecific Heat - .05	Density	    - .2	Electroneg. - 0.2Atomic Radius - .05 Ang.